a.If the implementation of generated or derived
classes differs only through a parameter we have
to use templates.
In C++ templates provide support for generic
programming. Template classes and functions
eliminate the code duplication of different data
types and make development easier and faster.
e.g. We can use more than one generic data type
in a class template, and each generic data type is
separated by the comma
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
template<class T1, class T2>
class A
{
T1 a;
T2 b;
public:
A(T1 x,T2 y)
{
a = x;
b = y;
}
void display()
{
std::cout << "Values of a and b are : " <<
a<<" ,"<<b<<std::endl;
}
};
int main()
{
A<int,float> d(5,6.5);
d.display();
return 0;
}
Output: Values of a and b are: 5,6.5
b.  If the actual types of objects used cannot be
known at compile time, then we have to use class
hierarchy
Class hierarchy allows us to use the concept of
polymorphism. Polymorphism means that a call
to a member function will cause a different
function to be executed depending on the type
of object that invokes the function.
c. If inline operations are essential and templates
are used then we have improved run-time
efficiency.
Runtime efficiency can be improved by using
inline functions and templates because here we
need not repeat function calls for different data
types used by the same functions or classes.
d. d.To gain access to differing instances for
derived classes through base we have to use
explicit casting
Explicit type casting is the process of converting
a value from one data type to another data type
explicitly. A derived object can be implicitly
converted to its base class. In contrast,
converting from the base class to the derived
class requires an explicit cast operator.
So option B is the correct answer.

do-while loop will execute the code block once,
before checking if the condition is true, then it
will repeat the loop as long as the condition is
true.
Syntax
do {
// code block to be executed
}
while (condition);
The example below uses a do/while loop. The
loop will always be executed at least once, even
if the condition is false, because the code block is
executed before the condition is tested:
Example
int i = 0;   // initialization is the first step
do {                              // execution of the body is
the second step
printf("%d\n", i);
i++;
}
while (i < 5);   // testing the condition is the third
step
